+# CIA decision, dissemination and preservation leads: independent source verification
+
+October 26, 2026. This check supports the core reported leads as statements in government reviews, while retaining unresolved originals, conflicting explanations and adverse findings. It adds eight linked research entries, CL001–CL008 / M153–M160, including one newly noticed low-weight elapsed-time difference. These are not eight independent evidence chains or findings of conspiracy.
+
+The most useful concrete preservation lead is CIA physical p.78 / printed p.53 footnote13: it reports the late-2002 CTC DUCS database deletion, uncertainty about when this draft was deleted, and recovery of a surviving January13 Lotus attachment in January2004. The same page describes an earlier instruction to retain this particular draft. All those statements must stay together.
+
+## Sources and what was actually checked
+
+The CIA document is the June2005 OIG report IG2003-0005-IN, with a visible release stamp dated March19,2015, C06184107. The held Black Vault mirror is 18,782,625 bytes and 491 physical pages, SHA-256 `28445bd38ccc03bf7ed9e9cb6ed309b303226152e76745098eb7bfa2230bbadb`. Its document date and release stamp are separate fields. The official CIA PDF request again returned a complete 508,740-byte maintenance HTML page rather than the report. The mirror was not byte-authenticated against an official-host PDF. No inference of deletion, tampering or deliberate removal follows from that access result.
+
+The official DOJ ChapterV HTML, official NARA MFR media PDF and official MFR index were freshly fetched. DOJ and the four-page MFR match their held copies exactly. The NARA item-to-media metadata crosswalk remains unavailable. See [source URLs, exact hashes and access receipts](SOURCES.json).
+
+Fourteen complete CIA page texts (physical2,17,69,77–83,105–108) and all four MFR pages were extracted and read. Eleven existing complete-page rasters were independently visually read: CIA2,17,78,79,105,106,108 and MFR1–4. Those render hashes were checked against receipts tied to the pinned source bytes. Selected DOJ paragraphs were read with surrounding sections; extraction of the full chapter is not a claim of substantive review of all of it. [Visual scope](VISUAL-REVIEW.json) is separate from the [text excerpts](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md) and their [pins](EXCERPT-PINS.json).
+
+An official review is a primary source for what its reviewers found, not a substitute for every historical record it describes. Two oversight reports sharing underlying material are not two additional eyewitnesses. No original CIR, hold order, SCALEC134684 cable, January13/20 email, native delivery receipt, deletion log or final accountability disposition was recovered here.
+
+## Findings and unresolved questions
+
+<a id="cl001"></a>
+### CL001 / M153 — A reported hold note and unanswered follow-up do not identify the original decision-maker
+
+**Type:** Source-supported review finding; original messages unacquired.
+
+CIA physical p.78 / printed p.53 reports a January 5, 2000 FBI detailee draft CIR addressed to FBI Headquarters and the New York Field Office, a targeting analyst's afternoon note attributing a hold instruction to the station's CIA-officer deputy chief, and a January 13 Lotus Note asking whether the draft should be remade. The review found no reply. DOJ III.A.4 separately reports the attributed note and unanswered mid-January inquiry.
+
+- The attribution in the analyst's note is not an acquired direct order, recording or authenticated identity of the actual decision-maker.
+- CIA pp.78 and80 / printed pp.53 and55 and DOJ III.A.4 describe access by FBI detailees. Failure to disseminate the formal CIR does not mean no FBI employee could access the underlying information.
+- Both reviews report failed formal dissemination. Repetition through related oversight reviews is not another independent contemporaneous witness.
+
+**Source locations:** CIA-MIRROR [78](https://documents.theblackvault.com/documents/cia/DOC_0006184107.pdf#page=78), [80](https://documents.theblackvault.com/documents/cia/DOC_0006184107.pdf#page=80); printed 53, 55 · [DOJ-CH5: III.A.4 and III.A.8.a (formal passage)](https://oig.justice.gov/sites/default/files/archive/special/0506/chapter5.htm); derivative [B082](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b082), [B083](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b083), [B084](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b084), [B085](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b085), [B086](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b086), [B087](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b087), [B139](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b139), [B140](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b140), [B141](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b141), [B142](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b142), [B143](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b143).
+
+**Next discriminator:** Obtain the native CIR, attributed hold annotation/version history, January 13 Lotus Note and any reply, release-authority records, access audits and actual intended-recipient delivery trail. Preserve pseudonyms and redactions; do not guess a real identity.
+
+**Limit:** The reports establish a documented non-dissemination/hold inquiry, not an attack-purpose or higher-level authorization finding.
+
+<a id="cl002"></a>
+### CL002 / M154 — SCALEC 134684 is an identifiable internal delivery claim with unverified external receipt
+
+**Type:** Source-supported cable identifier and unresolved delivery question.
+
+CIA physical p.79 / printed p.54 and its Appendix F5.b-1 physical p.108 / printed p.83 identify SCALEC 134684, dated January 5, 2000, as an internal cable claiming the travel documents had been passed to the FBI. Footnote14 says ALEC is the station's cable cite. The review could not identify the recipient or method, or confirm or refute successful delivery. It found no evidence of a later receipt check.
+
+- The claimed informal passage is distinct from the formal draft CIR that was not disseminated. One must not be silently substituted for the other.
+- DOJ III.A.6 and III.A.8.b describe an internal operational cable without recipient/method information, unable to corroborate the asserted passage. DOJ's assessment of the contemporary briefings is more negative than merely repeating the claim.
+- CIA p.77 / printed p.52 describes formal and informal channels in practice. That supplies an alternative route to investigate, not proof that this delivery succeeded.
+
+CIA physical p.81 / printed p.56 reports a January20 note saying the FBI had been kept abreast. It also says the review had no information of further communication with the FBI outside the station's detailees after January6. This is another review-described assertion and scope limit, not the acquired original January20 email.
+
+**Source locations:** CIA-MIRROR [77](https://documents.theblackvault.com/documents/cia/DOC_0006184107.pdf#page=77), [79](https://documents.theblackvault.com/documents/cia/DOC_0006184107.pdf#page=79), [81](https://documents.theblackvault.com/documents/cia/DOC_0006184107.pdf#page=81), [108](https://documents.theblackvault.com/documents/cia/DOC_0006184107.pdf#page=108); printed 52, 54, 56, 83 · [DOJ-CH5: III.A.6 and III.A.8.b (informal passage)](https://oig.justice.gov/sites/default/files/archive/special/0506/chapter5.htm); derivative [B095](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b095), [B096](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b096), [B097](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b097), [B098](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b098), [B099](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b099), [B145](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b145), [B146](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b146), [B147](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b147), [B148](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b148), [B149](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b149), [B150](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b150).
+
+**Next discriminator:** Recover the original SCALEC 134684 with attachments, routing/dissemination fields and referenced handoff; compare recipient-side incoming logs, contemporaneous briefing notes and any January 20 follow-up. No January 20 original was recovered here.
+
+**Limit:** A recorded assertion of delivery is not a receipt. The original cable was not acquired; no deliberate false entry is established.
+
+<a id="cl003"></a>
+### CL003 / M155 — DUCS database deletion, a retention instruction and a surviving Lotus copy are different preservation events
+
+**Type:** Source-supported preservation lead; deletion purpose and exact scope unverified.
+
+CIA physical p.78 / printed p.53 footnote13 says CTC deleted its DUCS database in late 2002 when DUCS was decommissioned. It expressly says the review did not know when this particular draft cable was ultimately deleted. The copy obtained in January 2004 was the version preserved in the January 13, 2000 Lotus Note.
+
+- The same page reports mid-February instructions to delete other dead cables while specifically retaining this draft CIR; DOJ III.A.4 likewise reports that retention request. Those instructions do not establish what was ultimately retained or migrated.
+- Database deletion is a reported event in a later review, not an acquired native deletion log. The surviving email copy rules out a claim that no copy survived, but does not establish completeness of all versions, attachments, backups or audits.
+- Decommissioning is the reported context. It does not itself establish lawful retention compliance, improper destruction or an intent to obstruct oversight.
+
+**Source locations:** CIA-MIRROR [78](https://documents.theblackvault.com/documents/cia/DOC_0006184107.pdf#page=78); printed 53 · [DOJ-CH5: III.A.4](https://oig.justice.gov/sites/default/files/archive/special/0506/chapter5.htm); derivative [B087](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b087).
+
+**Next discriminator:** Obtain DUCS decommissioning, migration, retention schedules/holds, backups and deletion/audit records; join them to the draft's versions, the mid-February retain instruction and the surviving Lotus attachment.
+
+**Limit:** No finding that every copy was destroyed, that this draft was selectively targeted, or that deletion concealed attack involvement.
+
+<a id="cl004"></a>
+### CL004 / M156 — CIA January and DOJ February 2004 discovery accounts require a version-and-recipient chronology
+
+**Type:** Attributable oversight chronology with unresolved source-version join.
+
+CIA physical p.78 / printed p.53 says its OIG obtained the Lotus-preserved draft only in January 2004. DOJ Chapter V II.1 says it noticed a previously undisclosed CIR title in February 2004 while reviewing a list of documents accessed by FBI employees at CIA; it also says CIA OIG had not previously obtained the document in connection with its review. DOJ says the belated discovery required more interviews/documents and delayed completion.
+
+- Preserve CIA's January date, DOJ's February date and DOJ's statement about CIA's previous acquisition together. The version, exact acquisition time and inter-review transmission are not joined by native receipts.
+- Different agencies, copies and discovery/receipt events could matter, but that is an untested possibility, not a reconciliation adopted here. Neither an established contradiction about one identical receipt nor a resolved non-conflict is claimed.
+- DOJ II.1 says it lacked independent CIA database access and initially could review but not retain copies of certain records. That is a documented completeness constraint, not proof of coordinated concealment.
+
+**Source locations:** CIA-MIRROR [78](https://documents.theblackvault.com/documents/cia/DOC_0006184107.pdf#page=78); printed 53 · [DOJ-CH5: II.1 OIG investigation](https://oig.justice.gov/sites/default/files/archive/special/0506/chapter5.htm); derivative [B015](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b015), [B017](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b017), [B018, official chapter](https://oig.justice.gov/sites/default/files/archive/special/0506/chapter5.htm), [B019](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b019), [B020](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b020).
+
+**Next discriminator:** Recover dated CIA/DOJ OIG requests, search and access lists, acquisition receipts, interview schedules, version/attachment hashes and cross-review production records for January–February 2004.
+
+**Limit:** The delay and search limitation are real reported oversight issues. The examined records do not identify the cause of delayed discovery or prove coordinated nonproduction.
+
+<a id="cl005"></a>
+### CL005 / M157 — A formatting explanation in the CIA summary is qualified by its detailed account and DOJ's assessment
+
+**Type:** Unresolved explanatory and evidentiary-status difference.
+
+CIA physical p.17 / printed p.xv says the message was apparently not sent because it was in the wrong format or needed editing. CIA physical p.79 / printed p.54 attributes major-editing concerns to an officer's speculation on reviewing a copy in early February 2004, after the account of participants' inability to recall the draft. DOJ III.A.8.a instead describes the information as documented in the appropriate format and says it could not determine why the CIR was not disseminated.
+
+- All three source formulations remain side by side: apparent formatting/editing explanation, retrospective editing speculation, and DOJ's appropriate-format/unknown-reason assessment.
+- No contemporaneous format rejection, editing instruction, revised draft or release decision was acquired. A later suggested reason cannot be silently promoted into the established January 2000 cause.
+- The wording difference does not itself establish a consciously false summary. Formatting, classification sanitization, release authority and delivery are separate questions for the original workflow records.
+
+DOJ IV.D's introductory assessment also says none of the evaluated FBI employees committed misconduct, although several fell short in performance. It leaves CIA personnel accountability to CIA OIG; neither that qualification nor the criticisms are omitted here.
+
+**Source locations:** CIA-MIRROR [17](https://documents.theblackvault.com/documents/cia/DOC_0006184107.pdf#page=17), [78](https://documents.theblackvault.com/documents/cia/DOC_0006184107.pdf#page=78), [79](https://documents.theblackvault.com/documents/cia/DOC_0006184107.pdf#page=79); printed xv, 53, 54 · [DOJ-CH5: III.A.8.a (formal passage)](https://oig.justice.gov/sites/default/files/archive/special/0506/chapter5.htm); derivative [B139](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b139), [B140](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b140), [B141](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b141), [B142](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b142), [B143](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b143).
+
+**Next discriminator:** Recover contemporaneous version histories, classification/editing reviews, release instructions and any rejection/rework record; compare the source basis used for the executive summary and detailed passage.
+
+**Limit:** A source-backed question about the asserted explanation and its evidentiary basis; not a finding of deliberate falsification or attack-purpose withholding.
+
+<a id="cl006"></a>
+### CL006 / M158 — No accountability recommendation on uncertain January passage is not general exoneration
+
+**Type:** Source-scope qualification and distinct affirmative failure findings.
+
+CIA physical pp.105–106 / printed pp.80–81 makes no accountability recommendation concerning January informal passage because it could neither confirm nor refute that passage. The same discussion reports a separate March 2000 failure to tell the FBI about Hazmi's US-bound departure and later missed opportunities. Page105 also recommends an Accountability Board assessment of systemic watchlisting management.
+
+- Uncertainty about one delivery issue is retained alongside affirmative findings on other failures; it is not recast as proof all conduct was proper.
+- The report's findings are not the final disposition of a later Accountability Board, discipline decision, criminal inquiry or historical-cause adjudication. Those later outcomes were not established in this pass.
+- DOJ's own accountability assessment distinguishes CIA responsibility, FBI detailee initiative and inadequate further follow-up. Those statements do not identify a single attack-motivated actor.
+
+DOJ IV.D's introductory assessment also says none of the evaluated FBI employees committed misconduct, although several fell short in performance. It leaves CIA personnel accountability to CIA OIG; neither that qualification nor the criticisms are omitted here.
+
+**Source locations:** CIA-MIRROR [17](https://documents.theblackvault.com/documents/cia/DOC_0006184107.pdf#page=17), [105](https://documents.theblackvault.com/documents/cia/DOC_0006184107.pdf#page=105), [106](https://documents.theblackvault.com/documents/cia/DOC_0006184107.pdf#page=106); printed xv, 80, 81 · [DOJ-CH5: IV.D.1 (Dwight)](https://oig.justice.gov/sites/default/files/archive/special/0506/chapter5.htm); derivative [B569](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b569), [B570](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b570), [B571](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b571), [B573](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b573), [B574](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b574), [B575](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b575).
+
+**Next discriminator:** Obtain the underlying March cables/access audits, action logs and the issue-specific accountability referral/response and final board or management dispositions. Do not infer an actual sanction from a recommendation.
+
+**Limit:** Supports an accountability inquiry, not a final finding of conspiracy, exoneration, criminal intent or WTC 7 involvement.
+
+<a id="cl007"></a>
+### CL007 / M159 — The December 16, 2003 redacted MFR is a bounded record lead, not an authenticated real-name or CIR join
+
+**Type:** Official-host source recovery and limited catalog association.
+
+The independently re-fetched NARA media PDF has four physical pages and matches the held copy byte-for-byte. Page1 gives a December16,2003 interview, December17 preparation, Team4, and FBI-headquarters location; it says the memorandum is not verbatim and is organized by subject. The official MFR index lists NAID2610319, [FBI Employee #2], FBI affiliation, December16,2003, four pages, redacted, box13.
+
+- These visible attributes are consistent with the index entry, but the catalog's item-to-media asset crosswalk was not retrieved. The filename/index association is not presented as a completed metadata-level join.
+- All four visible pages were independently read. The subject's identity and major ALEC/pre-9/11 passages remain redacted. Visible text does not establish this subject authored, held or received the January 2000 CIR.
+- No secondary site's real-name identification is adopted, and the redacted subject is not treated as an additional identified witness to the hold.
+
+**Source locations:** NARA-MFR [1](https://nara-media-001.s3.amazonaws.com/arcmedia/9-11/MFR/t-0148-911MFR-00724.pdf#page=1), [2](https://nara-media-001.s3.amazonaws.com/arcmedia/9-11/MFR/t-0148-911MFR-00724.pdf#page=2), [3](https://nara-media-001.s3.amazonaws.com/arcmedia/9-11/MFR/t-0148-911MFR-00724.pdf#page=3), [4](https://nara-media-001.s3.amazonaws.com/arcmedia/9-11/MFR/t-0148-911MFR-00724.pdf#page=4) · [NARA-INDEX](https://www.archives.gov/research/9-11/commission-memoranda.html): NAID2610319 row; [FBI Employee #2], 12/16/2003, four pages, R, box13.
+
+**Next discriminator:** Obtain the complete NARA catalog item/object mapping and authorized declassified underlying interview records. Test any claimed CIR/identity connection with explicit records rather than redaction reconstruction.
+
+**Limit:** The official-host PDF is verified as the bytes examined; the visible text supplies no direct proof of the proposed CIR decision chain or a named individual's participation.
+
+<a id="cl008"></a>
+### CL008 / M160 — Five-day, eight-day and January 13 follow-up descriptions remain distinct
+
+**Type:** Low-weight timing-description discrepancy noticed during this check.
+
+DOJ Chapter V IV.D.1 says the drafter inquired again five days later. Its III.A.4 narrative says that as of eight days later the CIR was still undispatched and describes a mid-January email; III.A.8.a says a week later. CIA physical p.78 / printed p.53 explicitly dates the draft January5 and the Lotus follow-up January13,2000.
+
+- The exact source phrases five days later, eight days later, a week later, and the January5/January13 dates are preserved. Simple subtraction of the named calendar dates gives eight days; it does not authenticate which underlying email the other summaries describe.
+- An approximation, a different reference point, another communication or an editorial error are untested possibilities. No date is corrected or selected as controlling.
+- The differing elapsed-time descriptions do not remove the reviews' shared finding that the formal CIR was not sent or establish deliberate alteration of the record.
+
+**Source locations:** [DOJ-CH5: III.A.4, III.A.8.a (formal passage), IV.D.1 (Dwight)](https://oig.justice.gov/sites/default/files/archive/special/0506/chapter5.htm); derivative [B086](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b086), [B140](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b140), [B574](SOURCE-EXCERPTS.md#doj-b574) · CIA-MIRROR [78](https://documents.theblackvault.com/documents/cia/DOC_0006184107.pdf#page=78); printed 53.
+
+**Next discriminator:** Obtain the native dated email(s), attachment versions, audit and response records, and compare the report's drafting/citation history. Keep separate any distinct follow-up events.
+
+**Limit:** A documentary precision lead with little independent bearing on motive or attack participation; it must not inflate the count of independent evidence chains.
+
+## Bearing on the investigation
+
+The findings support questions about permission to disseminate, unverifiable claims of passage, preservation of electronic records, oversight search completeness and the evidentiary basis of retrospective explanations. The strongest objection to a deliberate-conspiracy interpretation is that the acquired records are later reviews with missing originals, memory limitations and documented alternative channels; they do not establish the motive for the specific hold. Conversely, those limits do not erase affirmative findings of repeated information-sharing and operational failures or turn an unresolved delivery claim into proved receipt.
+
+The record can test institutional accountability without presupposing an attack-facilitation operation. Deliberate withholding, mistaken passage claims, editing/release problems, incomplete records and administrative failures remain hypotheses to discriminate with actual records; none is selected here. No link to WTC7 access, demolition preparation, manufactured attribution or a higher-level attack order is established. Identity guesses and secondary-site real-name identifications are excluded.
+
+All five BR bundles and ten bank/phone BPR targets remain in the preceding master. BR03 now has a review-report mirror available while its native draft/routing/recipient records remain absent. The earlier CIA maintenance-page observation remains true for that request and is not rewritten. New questions are tracked in [OPEN-QUESTIONS.json](OPEN-QUESTIONS.json).
+
+## Our own method issues and preservation
+
+The first worktree-selected Python lacked pypdf; an existing explicit Python3.13.7 runtime was used instead, with no installation. An initial HTML extractor repeated nested paragraphs; its output was preserved, and a separate leaf-block v2 extraction supplies all public DOJ block locators. Draft subsection citations were checked against official HTML anchors and corrected to III.A.6, III.A.8.a/b and IV.D.1 before publication, with its earlier draft and text diff retained locally. These are our workflow/citation errors, not historical evidence anomalies.
+
+Original source bytes, first derivatives, failed attempts, draft records and the other investigation's source study remain unchanged or separately preserved. No raw PDFs, HTML response bodies, raster images, contact/account data, private case material or credentials are in the public notes package. There was no outreach, fee, redaction reconstruction, legal-fact promotion or native historical-message authentication. Public-commit and remote-byte verification are recorded separately after the commit exists; precommit checks do not claim that publication has occurred.
